<ArticleLayout slug="beauty-content-production-china" ctaLabel="Plan a beauty launch" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Beauty is the most heavily regulated thing a studio can shoot in China, and almost none of that regulation is written about the shoot. It is written about the claim. Get the claim wrong and the frame it sits in becomes the evidence.</p>

  <h2>What does beauty content in China have to do differently?</h2>

  <p>Three things. Every efficacy claim has to match what the product is registered or filed to say, with published evidence behind it. Medical wording is banned outright, on the label and in the ad. Any depiction of a result has to be true, which is where retouching, before-and-after frames and generated skin stop being craft decisions and start being compliance ones. hubStudio produces beauty campaigns in China against these rules.</p>

  <p>This page describes production practice, not legal advice. Every instrument is cited so you can take it to a lawyer, which is what you should do before you sign anything that turns on it.</p>

  <p>Reviewed 18 September 2026.</p>

  <p>One thing to hold onto before the detail: the rules below sit at four different levels. There is national statute, there is national regulator instrument, there are local guidelines that bind in one city and persuade everywhere else, and there are platform rules that are stricter than any of them. Flattening that into "China bans it" is the mistake that costs a reshoot, because roughly half the strictest lines on this page are local or platform-level, and the other half are not.</p>

  <h2>What claims can a cosmetic make in China?</h2>

  <p>Start with the split that governs everything downstream. China sorts cosmetics by risk, and the riskier half has to be registered before it can make its claim at all.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Products for hair dyeing, perming, spot removal and whitening, sun protection and hair-loss prevention, and any product claiming a new efficacy, are special cosmetics, teshu huazhuangpin, which are registered with the drug regulator. All other cosmetics are filed.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cosmetics Supervision and Administration Regulation, State Council Order No. 727, Articles 4 and 16, in force 1 January 2021. Statutory text read on two government surfaces, September 2026.</p>

  <p>A general cosmetic that talks like a special one is not a copy problem. It is a registration problem that the copy exposed.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Cosmetics Supervision and Administration Regulation bars cosmetics advertising from stating or implying a medical effect, and bars false or misleading content that deceives or misleads consumers.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cosmetics Supervision and Administration Regulation, Article 43, published 29 June 2020, in force 1 January 2021. Statutory text read on the State Council release and a Beijing government portal, September 2026.</p>

  <p>The same ban runs through the labeling rules, and the label list is the more useful one for a production team because it names the mechanisms rather than the intent.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's cosmetics labeling rules, in force since 1 May 2022, bar a label from implying a medical effect through medical terms, the names of medical figures or drug names, or through trademarks, patterns, font color and size, homophones or symbols. They also bar false, exaggerated or absolute wording, fabricated or unverifiable research, statistics or surveys, using an ingredient's function to imply an efficacy the product lacks or may not claim, and any assertion or guarantee of efficacy or safety.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cosmetics Labeling Measures, National Medical Products Administration Announcement 2021 No. 77, Article 19, read in the State Council Gazette, September 2026. The article lists twelve prohibited items; this quote carries the ones that bear on content.</p>

  <p>Read that list as an art direction brief and it changes what you put on set. The rule reaches wardrobe and typography, not only copy. A white coat reads as a medical figure. So does a stethoscope left on the counter. Setting an ingredient name in a heavier weight than the claim around it is font size implying efficacy, which is one of the twelve items by name.</p>

