  <p>Penalties are worth knowing because they scale with repetition rather than with the size of the claim.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Outside medical, drug and medical device advertising, no ad in China may refer to a disease treatment function or use medical terms, or terms that confuse the product with a drug or a medical device. A breach draws a fine of one to three times the advertising fee, or 100,000 to 200,000 RMB where the fee cannot be calculated or is obviously low, rising to three to five times the fee, or 200,000 to 1,000,000 RMB, in serious cases.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Advertising Law of the People's Republic of China, Articles 17 and 58, as amended 29 April 2021. Statutory text read in full on the Ministry of Commerce law database, September 2026.</p>

  <h2>Which efficacy claims need an evaluation report?</h2>

  <p>The published-summary duty is the part foreign teams miss, because it has no equivalent in most markets. Holding the evidence is not enough. It has to be published where anyone who wants to check it can find it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Every efficacy claim needs a sufficient scientific basis, and the company that registers or files the product must publish a summary of the literature, research data or evaluation results behind the claim on a website the national drug regulator designates, for public scrutiny.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cosmetics Supervision and Administration Regulation, Article 22, in force 1 January 2021. Statutory text read on two government surfaces, September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Failing to publish that summary draws a fine of 10,000 to 30,000 RMB, rising to 30,000 to 50,000 RMB with suspension of production or operations in serious cases. A cosmetics ad that breaks the Regulation is penalized under the Advertising Law.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cosmetics Supervision and Administration Regulation, Articles 62 and 69, in force 1 January 2021. Statutory text read on a Beijing government portal on two separate occasions, September 2026.</p>

  <p>Which claims need which evidence comes from a separate instrument, and the grade of evidence steps up sharply for six of them.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's rules for evaluating cosmetics efficacy claims, in force since 1 May 2021, require a human efficacy test for six claims: spot removal and whitening, sun protection, hair-loss prevention, acne, nourishing and repair. Other claims may rest on evaluation tests, literature or research data analysis, as the claim requires.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Standard for Evaluating Cosmetics Efficacy Claims, National Medical Products Administration Announcement 2021 No. 50, published April 2021. The regulator's own page refused automated requests in September 2026, so the text was read on two independent regulatory reference surfaces that agree.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Claims a consumer can recognize directly by sight or smell, such as cleansing, makeup removal, makeup and fragrance, and effects that work purely physically, such as covering, are exempt from publishing an efficacy summary.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Standard for Evaluating Cosmetics Efficacy Claims, National Medical Products Administration Announcement 2021 No. 50, read on two independent regulatory reference surfaces, September 2026.</p>

  <p>A note on how that last pair is sourced, because it bears on how much weight to put on it. The regulator's own site returned an HTTP 412 to every automated request on two separate attempts eight days apart, so the standard is cited here by name and announcement number and never by article number. Anything turning on a specific article of that standard should be read from the instrument itself before you build to it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's cosmetics classification catalog sorts efficacy claims into 26 categories, plus a separate category for any new efficacy that fits none of them.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cosmetics Classification Rules and Catalog, National Medical Products Administration Announcement 2021 No. 49. Read through a legal portal's reproduction of the catalog, dated October 2022, and matched against two regulatory reference pages, September 2026.</p>

  <p>The practical consequence for a shoot: the script and the supers get locked against the filed claim set before the call sheet goes out, not after the edit. Post cannot fix a claim that was never filed.</p>

  <h2>Can beauty ads in China show before and after?</h2>

  <p>This is the question the English-language pages either skip or answer wrongly, usually by asserting a flat national ban that no instrument contains. The honest answer has layers, and the layers are the useful part.</p>

  <p>At national level there is no cosmetics-specific rule naming before-and-after imagery. There is something broader.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Fabricating the effect of using a product is one of the forms of false advertising China's Advertising Law names.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Advertising Law of the People's Republic of China, Article 28, as amended 29 April 2021. Statutory text read on the Ministry of Commerce law database, September 2026.</p>

  <p>The most precise language anywhere sits in a local guideline, and it opens by granting something rather than forbidding it. That opening sentence is the single most useful line in this whole area for a production team.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Shanghai's cosmetics advertising guideline opens its rule on showing product effects by allowing moderate artistic rendering of a cosmetic's efficacy. The same article then requires any depiction of use effects to be true, bars misleading consumers through excessive retouching, editing, software processing or special shooting techniques, and bars fabricated or edited before-and-after images or video.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shanghai Cosmetics Industry Advertising Compliance Guideline, Article 28, issued 28 February 2024 by the Shanghai Administration for Market Regulation and the Shanghai Medical Products Administration, read on the Shanghai government host of the text, September 2026. A local guideline, not a national rule.</p>

  <p>Moderate artistic rendering is allowed. Excessive processing that misleads is not. Fabricated or edited before-and-after footage is not. No instrument defines where moderate ends, so the working line a studio can actually hold is procedural rather than aesthetic: the result on screen has to be a result the product produced, and you should be able to show the unedited capture it came from.</p>

  <p>Beijing's guideline, issued more recently, does not address the question at all. Three separate reads of the city's text and its regulator's own interpretation confirmed the absence. That leaves the Shanghai wording short of a national consensus, and treating it as one overstates what has actually been written down.</p>

  <p>Enforcement, meanwhile, is real and small and instructive.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In February 2025 a regional market regulator published two cases against cosmetics sellers over before-and-after images. One advertised a hair product on a messaging platform with before-and-after pictures and the line "many white hairs before use, none after," and was fined 6,000 RMB for false advertising. The other posted on a short-video platform a face with obvious spots before a whitening mask and none after, alongside skin-improvement percentages with no stated source, and was fined 5,000 RMB.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region Administration for Market Regulation, ten typical illegal advertising cases, 21 February 2025. The regulator's own case bulletin, citing Advertising Law Articles 11 and 28 and Cosmetics Regulation Article 43. Two small regional cases, not a measure of national enforcement.</p>

  <p>Note what the second case was actually punished for. Not the image alone: the image plus three percentages with nothing behind them. Those two failures travel together in almost every beauty asset that gets pulled, and the percentages are the easier one to fix.</p>

  <p>One neighboring regime is stricter and frequently gets confused with this one.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's market regulator targets medical aesthetics ads that create appearance anxiety, use patients' names or images for before-and-after treatment comparisons, or use endorsers, and it counts "recommendation officers" and "experience officers" as endorsers. The same guidelines target cosmetics that promote disease treatment functions tied to medical aesthetics.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: State Administration for Market Regulation, Enforcement Guidelines on Medical Aesthetics Advertising, Announcement 2021 No. 37, 1 November 2021, read on two government-hosted copies, September 2026.</p>

  <p>If the brand sits next to a clinic, as post-procedure skincare does, the medical aesthetics guideline is the one that bites, and patient before-and-after is out.</p>

  <h2>What do the rules say about models and endorsers?</h2>

  <p>No instrument read addresses models as such. Three separate rules reach them anyway, and together they decide what a beauty casting and a beauty release have to cover.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">An endorser in China may not recommend or vouch for a product they have not used, and no child under ten may be an endorser. An endorser in breach forfeits the illegal income and is fined one to two times that amount.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Advertising Law of the People's Republic of China, Articles 38 and 61, as amended 29 April 2021. Statutory text read on the Ministry of Commerce law database, September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Beijing's market and medical products regulators require cosmetics ad claims to match the product's registration or filing and its published efficacy summary. Their guideline bars ads from creating appearance anxiety or using medical terms, bars general cosmetics from claiming the efficacy of special cosmetics, and says endorsers must be at least ten and may only recommend cosmetics they have used.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Beijing Cosmetics Advertising Guideline, issued 27 June 2025 by the Beijing Administration for Market Regulation and the Beijing Medical Products Administration, read on the city's government portal and the regulator's own interpretation, September 2026. A local guideline, not a national rule.</p>

  <p>Then the likeness itself. Generated and edited faces change the paperwork here.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under China's Civil Code, a likeness may not be made, used or published without the portrayed person's consent; where a license clause is disputed it is construed in favor of the person portrayed; and where the license term is unagreed or unclear either party may end it on reasonable notice.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Civil Code of the People's Republic of China, Articles 1019, 1021 and 1022, in force 1 January 2021. Statutory text.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under China's deep synthesis provisions, in force since 10 January 2023, a provider offering face or voice editing must prompt the user to inform the person being edited and obtain that person's separate consent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the Provisions on the Administration of Deep Synthesis Internet Information Services, Article 14, issued by the Cyberspace Administration of China, the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology and the Ministry of Public Security, read on the regulator's site, September 2026.</p>

  <p>Separate consent is the operative phrase. A model release that covers the shoot does not automatically cover editing that model's face afterward, and on a beauty job face editing is not an edge case, it is the work.</p>

  <h2>Who has to mark a beauty post as an advertisement?</h2>

  <p>Seeding content, zhongcao, is the backbone of beauty in China, and it is advertising when it carries a way to buy.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under China's Measures for the Administration of Internet Advertising, in force 1 May 2023, an advertisement must be identifiable as one, and any post that promotes goods through knowledge introductions, experience sharing or consumer reviews while carrying a purchase link must be conspicuously marked "advertisement."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Measures for the Administration of Internet Advertising, Article 9, State Administration for Market Regulation Order No. 72, issued 25 February 2023, third paragraph read verbatim, September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same Measures put advertiser duties on a seller who promotes by livestream, and advertising operator or publisher duties on the room operator providing design, production, agency or publishing services.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Measures for the Administration of Internet Advertising, Article 19, in force 1 May 2023. Statutory text read verbatim, September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Shanghai's cosmetics advertising guideline applies those internet advertising rules to seeding posts, reviews and store-visit content used to advertise cosmetics on internet platforms.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Shanghai Cosmetics Industry Advertising Compliance Guideline, Article 29, issued 28 February 2024, read on two hosts of the text, September 2026. A local guideline, not a national rule.</p>

  <p>The production consequence is a deliverable, not a policy. If a creator asset can carry a purchase link, the disclosure mark belongs in the layout from the first layout, sized to survive the platform's own crop.</p>

  <h2>What gets a beauty asset rejected in platform review?</h2>

  <p>Platform rules are stricter than the instruments, and they are also the hardest to read. Here is exactly what was and was not reachable in September 2026, because a page claiming more than this is guessing.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A beauty industry content review manual on a Chinese creator-marketing platform's help center, dated September 2026, bars creator content from medical-effect wording, from comparing a product with medical aesthetics treatments, from claims such as "suits all skin types," and from wrinkle and aging wording. It requires authorization for a celebrity's likeness and proof for any data, patent or award a post cites.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Xingtu marketing service platform help center, beauty industry content review manual, page dated 2 September 2026, read unauthenticated on two occasions in September 2026. A primary reading for that page's scope only.</p>

  <p>That manual carries the sharpest lesson on the page. Anti-wrinkle is a filed category in the national catalog, something a product can lawfully be registered to claim, and wrinkle wording is still constrained in creator content on that surface. Clearing the regulator does not clear platform review, and the two get checked by different people at different times.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Douyin's ecommerce beauty promotion standards, as revised in May 2022, barred medical-effect wording from cosmetics promotion, along with the names of medical aesthetics procedures such as whitening injections, skin-booster injections and botulinum toxin.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Chinese ecommerce trade press and a web-services repost reporting the platform's revised standards, in force 17 May 2022, read September 2026. The platform's rule center was not reached, and the current version may differ.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">RedNote's community standards page returned no rule text to an unauthenticated request on two dates in September 2026, matching four earlier requests to its creator, advertiser, help and school surfaces.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: direct unauthenticated fetches of RedNote's own surfaces, September 2026, response content recorded per request. A primary observation, not a rule.</p>

  <p>So: one platform surface readable and current, one readable only through four-year-old press, one not readable at all. RedNote's beauty rules were not reached, which is not the same as saying it has none. Assume they exist and get them from inside the account before a beauty batch goes up.</p>

  <p>On rejection reasons, a caveat worth stating plainly. No platform rejection notice was obtained for this page, and no source ranks rejection grounds by frequency. What the table below carries is the grounds the regulator and the platform manual state, not a count of what actually gets pulled most often.</p>

