  <h2>Does retouched or generated skin break a platform rule?</h2>

  <p>One marketplace has written a rule that reaches this directly, and beauty is the category where it bites hardest, because skin is exactly what a generative model is best at improving and exactly what the customer will compare against the mirror.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Chinese marketplace published what was reported as the sector's first rule aimed specifically at AI fake product images, in force 27 March 2025, naming four failure categories: product material or style that does not match the real item, distorted or exaggerated product effects, an obvious cutout and paste look, and bodies or scenes that break physical laws. It is enforced by a detection model that intercepts images at upload, and the platform said a notice marking AI synthesis would appear on product detail pages.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Science and Technology Daily, 28 March 2025, reporting Taobao's own rule announcement and its detection-model enforcement.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Applied to beauty, a retouched or generated skin result that shows more than the product delivers falls into that rule's "distorted or exaggerated product effects" category. The rule carries no clause about skin; this is a reading of its categories.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a reading of the Taobao AI fake-image rule's published categories, September 2026. This describes production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <p>Stack that against Shanghai's Article 28 and the two rules point the same way from opposite directions. One says an exaggerated effect is a fake image. The other says excessive processing of a use effect misleads. Neither cares whether a camera or a model made the pixels.</p>

  <p>Then there is the labeling duty, which is separate again and applies whatever the content shows.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's rules on labeling AI-generated content, in force since 1 September 2025, call for a visible label on generated images and video, an implicit label in the file's metadata, and a visible label that stays on exported or downloaded copies.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the Measures for Labeling AI-Generated Synthetic Content, issued 14 March 2025 by the Cyberspace Administration of China and three other national bodies, read on the regulator's site, September 2026.</p>

  <p>How those duties fall between a brand, its studio and its tool vendor is generally treated as a matter for the contract rather than settled by the Measures themselves. The studio-side habit that survives every reading of it: label at export, keep the metadata, and keep the generation record per asset. hubStudio's article on <a href="/resources/insights/china-ai-labeling-rules-production-workflow">China's AI labeling rules</a> walks that pipeline stage by stage.</p>

  <h2>How to read these numbers</h2>

  <p>The deliverables below are a different kind of information from everything above, and they are weaker. The rules above are read from instruments. These are counted from published sources.</p>

  <p>These specs are not read from a seller backend. Tmall's rule center gates its rule text behind a seller login, so no open source reaches the official value. For Douyin and RedNote, "seller backend" is the wrong phrase and rule centers are not what failed: those platforms' own creator and help surfaces serve no specification text to anyone not logged in, so no open source reaches the official value for the app.</p>

  <p>What follows is the most commonly published figure for each spec, collected across independent sources in Chinese and English on 10 September 2026, with the number of sources that agreed shown per row. The pools differ by platform, 18 sources for the Tmall rows, 12 for Douyin and 20 for RedNote, which is why the count is printed per row rather than once for the table. Rows marked consensus had broad agreement. Rows marked contested did not, and those carry the full spread rather than a single number.</p>

  <p>Treat this as the best available public reading, not as the rule. Platform specs change without announcement and often without an English notice at all. Before you build a batch to any number on this page, open your own creator account or seller backend and confirm it there. Reviewed 18 September 2026.</p>

  <p>Some platform surfaces are readable, and it is worth being precise about which, because none of them fed the table below. The Xingtu beauty content review manual is readable without a login and is cited on this page for content review grounds only, labeled primary for that scope alone. Douyin's upload API documentation is readable and covers the API route rather than creative specification. Neither supplied a single value in the deliverables table, and no readable platform surface published a beauty-specific slot map at all.</p>

  <h2>What does a beauty SKU need on Tmall, Douyin and RedNote?</h2>

  <p>The parts of a listing are stable across every reference checked. The numbers attached to them are not.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Every Chinese production reference checked builds a listing from the same named parts: a main image set with a pure-white-background image inside it, a long scrolling detail page, a separate mobile detail page, a size and specification chart, store decoration banners, and short video. The parts are stable. The dimensions attached to them are not.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eight independent Chinese design, tool and seller-education references collected September 2026, compared on their deliverable lists only, with every number they carried set aside.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
