  <p>Two rows deserve a flag rather than a footnote.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">There is no published Tmall Global image delta to work from. Sixteen of eighteen sources carrying a Tmall image figure do not distinguish the cross-border channel from the domestic one at any point. The two that do give one figure each, each on a single source: a 15-image maximum with a 1600 by 1600 minimum, and a cross-border cosmetics slot map of five images plus one video. Neither is corroborated anywhere else in the pool.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: eighteen independent published specifications collected 10 September 2026, in Chinese and English, counted for whether they distinguish the two channels. Stated as an absence with its count, not as a platform rule.</p>

  <p>That absence lands hardest on beauty, because imported cosmetics are one of the categories most likely to run through the cross-border channel. The beauty-specific slot map exists on exactly one source, which makes it a lead to verify in your own backend, not a spec to build to.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published sources do not agree on how many images one RedNote note carries. Three 2026 specification tables give 18. Two undated seller-education pages give 9. No source dates the change or explains it, so the honest reading is a range rather than a number.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: five independent published references collected 10 September 2026, counted per domain, range published rather than averaged. Not a platform reading.</p>

  <p>Build the carousel to nine and you are safe under either reading. The <a href="/solutions/platforms/tmall">Tmall platform page</a>, the <a href="/solutions/platforms/rednote">RedNote platform page</a> and hubStudio's <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">ecommerce design service page</a> carry the wider listing architecture these slots sit inside.</p>

  <h2>The pre-shoot compliance checklist</h2>

  <p>Run this before the call sheet goes out, not in the edit. Each line names the instrument behind it.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Claims locked to the filing.</strong> Every on-image and scripted claim mapped to the product's registration or filing and its published efficacy summary. Regulation Article 22, plus the Beijing and Shanghai guidelines.</li>
    <li><strong>Special claims on special products only.</strong> Whitening, spot removal, sun protection and hair-loss claims appear only on registered special cosmetics. Regulation Article 16.</li>
    <li><strong>No medical vocabulary anywhere, including the set.</strong> Copy, supers, props and wardrobe. Advertising Law Article 17, Labeling Measures Article 19, and the platform manual.</li>
    <li><strong>Before-and-after from unedited capture only.</strong> Keep the retouch log. The log is practice, not law. Advertising Law Article 28 and Shanghai Article 28.</li>
    <li><strong>Every percentage carries its source on the asset.</strong> Advertising Law Article 11, and the reason one of the two 2025 cases was fined.</li>
    <li><strong>Endorsers have used the product and are ten or older.</strong> Record the use. Advertising Law Article 38.</li>
    <li><strong>Likeness consent covers China, the platforms and the term.</strong> Civil Code Articles 1019, 1021 and 1022, with separate consent for face or voice editing under deep synthesis Article 14.</li>
    <li><strong>Generated or edited skin labeled, and honest.</strong> The explicit and implicit labels under the CAC Measures, and never showing more than the product does.</li>
    <li><strong>Purchase-link posts marked "advertisement."</strong> Livestream roles assigned before the room opens. Internet Advertising Measures Articles 9 and 19.</li>
    <li><strong>Authorizations and proof ready for review.</strong> Celebrity portrait and IP authorizations, plus documentation for any data, patent or award. The platform manual.</li>
  </ul>

